1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 e5 (5... O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 Nh5
8. Qd2 Qh4+ 9. g3 (9. Kd1 $5) 9... Nxg3 (9... Qe7 {Karpov-Kasparov, World
Championship, Lyon 1990 (1-0)}) 10. Qf2 Nxf1 11. Qxh4 Nxe3 12. Ke2 (12. Kf2
Nxc4 13. b3 Nb6 14. Nge2 Na6 15. Rhg1 f5 {transposes to Spassky-Bronstein,
given below}) 12... Nxc4 13. Rc1 {(Levitt-Martin, Glasgow 1989)} Bd7 14. b3 Nb6
{Levitt-Parker, Hastings 1991/2} (14... Na3 $5 {Gallagher})) 6. d5 Nh5 7. Be3
Na6 8. Qd2 ({"Instead of 8.Qd2, White could also try} 8. a3 {followed by a
quick b4 to try to show that the knight committed itself too early" (Joe
Gallagher, The Saemisch King's Indian (Batsford, 1995))}) 8... Qh4+ 9. g3 (9.
Bf2 Qe7 10. Nge2 f5 {Lein,A (2475)-Van der Wiel,J (2515), New Brunswick 1984 
(½-½, 25 moves)}) 9... Nxg3 $5 {Bronstein's novelty.} 10. Qf2 Nxf1 11. Qxh4
Nxe3 12. Kf2 (12. Ke2 $1 {is Joe Gallagher's annotation in The Saemisch King's
Indian (Batsford, 1995) but he gives nothing more than the exclamation mark})
12... Nxc4 13. b3 Na3 $1 ({Suggested by Bronstein after playing} 13... Nb6 {
in the game Spassky-Bronstein, Amsterdam 1956}) 14. Nge2 Nc5 15. Raf1 (15. Rad1
a5 16. Rhg1 O-O 17. Kg2 f5 18. Kh1 Nc2 19. Rd2 Nb4 20. Na4 Ncd3 21. Ng3 Bf6 22.
Qh6 Bg7 23. Qh4 {½-½, Kluger,G-Barden,L Hastings 1958, three months
previously.}) 15... O-O 16. Rhg1 f5 17. Kg2 Nc2 18. Kh1 Ne3 19. Rc1 Nd3 20. Nd1
$2 (20. Rcf1 $5) (20. Rb1 $4 Bf6 $3 $19) 20... Nxc1 21. Nxc1 Nxd1 22. Rxd1 fxe4
23. fxe4 Bd7 24. Ne2 Rf7 25. Qg3 Raf8 26. Kg2 Bh6 27. h3 Bb5 28. Nc3 Bf1+ {
Sources: CHESS, June 1958, p241; BCM, June 1958, p155; The Delights of Chess
by Assiac [Heinrich Fraenkel], Dover, 1974, p177} (28... Bf1+ 29. Rxf1 Rxf1 30.
Nb5 Bd2 $1 31. Nxc7 Be1 $19) 0-1

