{"It is fairly certain that this game will not be published anywhere else, for 239 other games besides were played in the Stevenson Memorial tournament, and these two protagonists finished respectively forty-fifth and thirty-sixth in the final table. This very fact, however, and the quality of the play, speak well for the strength of the event. A. T. Watson is a Brighton player who has adorned club and congress chess for over half-a-century, perennially cheery, occasionally brilliant and some�how managing never to look a day older than forty throughout." (BH Wood)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O b5 6. Bb3 Bc5 7. Qe2 d6 8. h3 Be6 9. Nc3 Na5 10. d3 Nxb3 11. axb3 c6 12. Be3 Bb6 13. d4 exd4 14. Nxd4 c5 $2 {"Infringing the simple old principle " Develop your pieces ! " I cannot see the least reason why he should not castle; it would have saved him a lot of pain." (BH Wood)} ({BHW overlooks the fact that, after} 14... O-O {, White can simply take a pawn with} 15. Nxc6 {JS}) 15. Nxe6 fxe6 16. Rxa6 $1 b4 17. Qb5+ Nd7 18. Rxa8 Qxa8 19. Na4 Qb8 20. Ra1 Bc7 21. Qc6 Ke7 22. Bg5+ Nf6 23. e5 dxe5 24. Nxc5 Bd6 25. Qd7+ {Source: Illustrated London News, 10 May 1958. Biographical note: Arthur Tijou Watson (1892, Hackney, London - 20 January 1969, Worthing, Sussex).} 1-0

